## Best Practices

### 1. Design for Query Patterns

```bash
# Query pattern: Find user by email
# Solution: Use GSI with email as partition key

# Query pattern: Get all user orders by date
# Solution: Use composite key (user_id + order_id) with LSI on created_at

# Query pattern: Get top sellers by revenue
# Solution: Use GSI with store_id + revenue as sort key
```

### 2. Partition Key Design

```bash
# Good: User ID (evenly distributed, high cardinality)
user_id: "user-123"

# Bad: Country (uneven distribution, low cardinality)
country: "US"  # Creates hot partitions

# Good: Composite (evenly distributed)
tenant_id#year_month: "tenant-001#2025-11"
```

### 3. Data Modeling

```bash
# Denormalization: Store related data together
{
  "user_id": "user-123",
  "name": "John",
  "profile": {
    "age": 30,
    "city": "NYC"
  },
  "latest_orders": [
    {"order_id": "123", "amount": 99.99},
    {"order_id": "124", "amount": 149.99}
  ]
}

# Advantages:
# - Single read operation
# - No joins needed
# - Faster queries
```

### 4. Capacity Planning

```bash
# Estimate WCU for provisioned mode:
# If writing 1,000 items/sec with avg 2 KB per item:
# 1,000 * (2 KB / 1 KB) = 2,000 WCU

# Estimate RCU for provisioned mode:
# If reading 5,000 items/sec with avg 4 KB per item (strongly consistent):
# 5,000 * (4 KB / 4 KB) = 5,000 RCU

# Use on-demand for unpredictable workloads
```

### 5. Security

```bash
# Enable encryption at rest
aws dynamodb update-table \
  --table-name Users \
  --sse-specification Enabled=true,SSEType=KMS

# Use IAM for access control
{
  "Version": "2012-10-17",
  "Statement": [
    {
      "Effect": "Allow",
      "Action": ["dynamodb:Query", "dynamodb:Scan"],
      "Resource": "arn:aws:dynamodb:us-east-1:123456789012:table/Users",
      "Condition": {
        "StringEquals": {
          "aws:userid": "user-123"
        }
      }
    }
  ]
}
```

### 6. Monitoring and Performance

```bash
# Set CloudWatch alarms for capacity exceeded
aws cloudwatch put-metric-alarm \
  --alarm-name DynamoDB-CapacityExceeded \
  --metric-name ConsumedWriteCapacityUnits \
  --namespace AWS/DynamoDB \
  --statistic Sum \
  --threshold 8000 \
  --comparison-operator GreaterThanThreshold

# Use CloudWatch Logs Insights to analyze queries
# Monitor throttling and adjust capacity
# Use DynamoDB Dashboard for performance metrics
```

